This article summarises results of the [[2020 New Zealand general election|17 October 2020 New Zealand general election]], including both [[party vote]] and [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] vote outcomes.

== Release of results ==
Preliminary results were released gradually after polling booths closed at 19:00 ([[New Zealand Time|NZDT]]) on 17 October.<ref name="about the election">{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/voting/get-ready-to-vote/about-the-2020-general-election/|title=About the 2020 General Election|website=vote.nz|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]|accessdate=9 September 2020|archive-date=10 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201010015057/https://vote.nz/voting/get-ready-to-vote/about-the-2020-general-election/|url-status=dead}}</ref> The preliminary count only includes advance ordinary and election day ordinary votes; it does not include any [[special vote]]s, which have a deadline ten days later (27 October).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/about-elections/how-are-general-election-votes-counted/|title=How are general election votes counted?|website=Elections.nz|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]|accessdate=10 September 2020}}</ref> Special votes include votes from those who enrolled after the deadline of 13 September, those who voted outside their electorate (including all overseas votes), voters in hospital or prison, and those voters enrolled on the unpublished roll.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/voting/get-help-to-vote/cant-get-to-a-voting-place/|title=Can't get to a voting place?|website=Elections.nz|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]|accessdate=10 September 2020}}</ref> The votes in the [[2020 New Zealand cannabis referendum|cannabis referendum]] and the [[2020 New Zealand euthanasia referendum|euthanasia referendum]] were counted after election day, with preliminary results released on 30 October.<ref name="about the election" />

All voting papers, counterfoils and electoral rolls are returned to the electorate's returning officer for a mandatory recount; this also includes approving and counting any special votes, and compiling a master roll to ensure no voter has voted more than once. To simplify processing and counting, overseas votes will be sent to and counted at the Electoral Commission's central processing centre in [[Wellington]], rather than to electorate returning officers.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Party Secretary Handbook – 2020 General Election and Referendums|url=https://elections.nz/assets/Handbooks/Party-Secretary-Handbook-June-2020.pdf|website=Elections.nz|publisher=Electoral Commission}}</ref> Official results, including all recounted ordinary votes and special votes, as well as the official results of the two referendums, are expected to be released by the Electoral Commission on 6 November 2020.<ref name="about the election" />

Parties and candidates have three working days after the release of the official results to apply for a [[Election recount|judicial recount]]. These recounts take place under the auspices of a [[District Court of New Zealand|District Court]] judge (the Chief District Court Judge in case of a nationwide recount),<ref>[[Electoral Act 1993]], sections 190 and 191.</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/about-elections/election-recounts-and-petitions/|title=Election recounts and petitions|website=Elections.nz|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]|accessdate=10 September 2020}}</ref> and may delay the return of the election writ by a few days.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/get-involved/features-pre-2016/document/00NZPHomeNews201408201/writ-day|title=Writ Day – New Zealand Parliament|website=Parliament.nz|publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]]|accessdate=10 September 2020}}</ref>

